ta: Relationships (12936)]. Although such combinations may be clinically justified in severe infections—especially in immunocompromised patients—the substantial toxicity risk necessitates frequent monitoring of renal and auditory function [Data: Relationships (12936)]. This synergy exemplifies how pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interactions can convert otherwise manageable therapies into high-risk regimens.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"补肾药物 may paradoxically contribute to kidney injury\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Despite their intended therapeutic purpose, 补肾药物 (kidney-tonifying drugs) are associated with 肾脏中毒 in unstructured clinical reports. The evidence suggests that some of these agents inhibit renal membrane transporters, leading to intracellular drug accumulation in renal tubular cells and subsequent nephrotoxicity. Moreover, inappropriate or excessive use of such drugs can impose unnecessary metabolic burdens on the kidneys, potentially triggering adverse drug reactions [Data: Relationships (16137)]. This finding cautions against the uncritical use of traditional or complementary medicines labeled as 'kidney-protective' without pharmacokinetic evaluation or renal monitoring.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Hearing loss is a critical adverse effect tied to specific diuretic–antibiotic combinations\",\n            \"explanation\": \"听力丧失 (hearing loss) emerges as a severe and potentially irreversible adverse reaction primarily linked to the use of 利尿酸 and 速尿—especially in renally impaired patients receiving high intravenous doses. The risk escalates dramatically when these loop diuretics are combined with aminoglycoside antibiotics, due to additive ototoxic effects on the cochlea [Data: Relationships (9256)]. Notably, this toxicity can manifest without warning vestibular symptoms, making early detection difficult [Data: Relationships (12936)]. This underscores the importance of avoiding such combinations 